personal name Liu Shi, was an emperor of the Chinese Han dynasty. He reigned from 48 BC to 33 BC. Emperor Yuan promoted Confucianism as the official creed of the Chinese government. He appointed adherents of Confucius to important government posts.", "newValue": "Emperor Yuan of Han, born Liu Shi, reigned from 48 BCE to 33 BCE, marking a critical juncture in the ideological evolution of the Western Han dynasty. His legacy is indelibly tied to the systematic promotion of Confucianism, which he elevated from a respected philosophy to the official state creed. This policy fundamentally reoriented the bureaucratic and intellectual framework of the empire, setting a precedent that influenced Chinese governance and culture for over two millennia. Yuan's reign thus signifies a profound institutional and philosophical transformation within one of history's most enduring empires.

During his sixteen-year rule, Emperor Yuan actively implemented Confucian principles across the imperial administration. He appointed numerous Confucian scholars, particularly those from the Guliang school, to key government posts, often at the expense of officials trained in Legalist or syncretic traditions. This shift fostered a court environment where classical learning, ritual propriety, and moral rectitude, as defined by Confucian texts, gained paramount importance. Notable figures like Xiao Wangzhi, who championed Confucian ethics, found favor, while more pragmatic or militarily-minded officials often faced demotion or dismissal, reflecting the emperor's deep personal conviction.

The institutionalization of Confucianism under Emperor Yuan had far-reaching consequences, solidifying its role as the bedrock of Chinese imperial ideology. It cultivated a scholar-official class whose education and values were rooted in Confucian classics, thereby shaping the criteria for bureaucratic recruitment and moral leadership for successive dynasties. This ideological entrenchment also contributed to a perceived weakening of central authority in later years, as some Confucian factions prioritized moral purity over practical governance, inadvertently paving the way for internal instability and the rise of powerful consort clans and regents."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-15T07:04:03Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31869350067", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "74 BCE, Chang'an"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "74 BCE, Chang'an"}], "newValue": ["The intellectual ferment of the Warring States period had established Confucianism as a prominent philosophical school, laying groundwork for its later adoption.", "Prior Han emperors, including Emperor Wu, had already initiated efforts to incorporate Confucian scholars into government, although not yet as the sole state ideology.", "Emperor Yuan's personal upbringing and education instilled in him a strong inclination towards Confucian moral philosophy and its emphasis on ritual and virtue."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-15T07:04:03Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#31869350067", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "32 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "32 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Emperor Yua